agent: Refuse connections from where agents' work runs

On a machine whose agents run as the person's own account, the socket,
open to the account, serves them every key the agent holds. Read a
gate, a file named by SSH_TPM_AGENT_GATE, at each connection, and refuse
a peer when the first process of its chain, itself and then each parent
up to init, whose cgroup a rule names is refused there. Allow ends the
search, so a place can be allowed inside a slice that is refused. A
refused peer hears the protocol's bare failure for every request, and
the journal says why, naming the place refused and the peer's folder.
watch journals what would be refused and serves it. A gate this agent
never read whole, whether gone, unreadable, unparsable or refusing no
place, refuses everyone. Only a regular file of at most 64 KiB is read,
opened without waiting, so a pipe or a device put at its path counts as
unreadable rather than stalling every connection. The journal says at
each start which gate the agent judges by, or that it has none.
Refused too is a peer the kernel doesn't name, one whose chain can't be
read to init, or one gone by the time it's judged, as its pidfd says. A file read once and gone, unreadable or unparsable
since, as a rollback past the gate or a later gate's format leaves it,
is judged by what it last said, journalled once as that starts and once
as a read comes back. Where the variable is unset, nothing changes.

Where systemd hands the agent's output to the journal, as
JOURNAL_STREAM says by the stream's device and inode, matched against
standard output's so one run from a terminal a unit started isn't
taken for it, each line opens with the sd-daemon mark of its
level's priority, so a refusal, or a gate refusing everyone, reaches
what reads the journal's warnings rather than standing as info.

